# Day 03 – Active Directory Structure: OUs, Users, and Groups

## 🎯 Purpose
Design and implement a logical directory hierarchy to organize network objects, enable delegated administration, and establish Role-Based Access Control (RBAC) using Groups.

---

## 🔑 Key Concepts & Terms

* Organizational Unit (OU): A container object in Active Directory used to organize users, computers, and other groups into a manageable hierarchy. OUs are the primary targets for Group Policy Objects (GPOs).
* Security Group: A collection of user or computer accounts used to assign permissions to shared resources (like folders or printers).
* Distinguished Name (DN): The absolute path to an object in the Active Directory database (e.g., `CN=Jane Doe,OU=HR,OU=_company,DC=corp,DC=local`).
* Security Identifier (SID): A unique, immutable alphanumeric string assigned to every user, group, and computer. Permissions are tied to the SID, not the username.
* dsa.msc: The command-line shortcut to open the "Active Directory Users and Computers" (ADUC) management console.

---

## 🏗️ Architecture: AD Hierarchy & RBAC

```text
corp.local (Root Domain)
│
└── _Company (Top-Level OU)
    │
    ├── IT (Child OU)
    │   └── Admin User (User Object)
    │
    ├── HR (Child OU)
    │   ├── Jane Doe (User Object)
    │   └── HR-Folder-Access (Security Group)
    │
    └── Sales (Child OU)
```
*Rule of thumb:* Users go into Groups, Groups get assigned Permissions to folders/resources. Never assign permissions directly to a user account.

