## 🛠️ Execution Methods

### Option A: Interactive GUI Method (ADUC)
1. Press `Win + R`, type `dsa.msc`, and press Enter.
2. Right-click the domain -> New -> Organizational Unit (Create `_Company`).
3. Right-click `_Company` -> New -> Organizational Unit (Create `IT`, `HR`).
4. Right-click the target OU -> New -> User (Follow wizard to set name and password).
5. Right-click the target OU -> New -> Group (Create a Global Security Group).
6. Double-click the Group -> Members tab -> Add -> Type username -> OK.

### Option B: PowerShell Automation (Enterprise Standard)

# 1. Create the OU Structure
New-ADOrganizationalUnit -Name "_Company" -Path "DC=corp,DC=local"
New-ADOrganizationalUnit -Name "HR" -Path "OU=_Company,DC=corp,DC=local"

# 2. Create a User Account
New-ADUser -Name "Jane Doe" -GivenName "Jane" -Surname "Doe" -SamAccountName "jane.doe" -UserPrincipalName "jane.doe@corp.local" -Path "OU=HR,OU=_Company,DC=corp,DC=local" -AccountPassword (ConvertTo-SecureString "P@ssw0rd123!" -AsPlainText -Force) -Enabled $true

# 3. Create a Security Group and Add the User
New-ADGroup -Name "HR-Folder-Access" -GroupScope Global -GroupCategory Security -Path "OU=HR,OU=_Company,DC=corp,DC=local"
Add-ADGroupMember -Identity "HR-Folder-Access" -Members "jane.doe"

---

## 💡 Real-World Explanation

If you dump all users and computers into the default Active Directory folders, you cannot apply specific security policies to different departments. 

By structuring the company into OUs, a SysAdmin can apply a policy that says, "Force the IT department's computer screens to lock after 5 minutes," without affecting the HR department. 

Furthermore, we use Security Groups for permissions. If HR has 50 employees, we don't add 50 individual users to the HR shared folder permissions. We add the `HR-Folder-Access` group to the folder once, and simply drop the 50 users into that group. When someone quits, we remove their account from the group, cleanly revoking all their access at once.

